# Packages installed with --no-install-recommends in stage-arlowe.
#
# This file MUST live inside a sub-stage directory. pi-gen's run_stage()
# iterates only directories under the stage root and reads NN-packages-nr
# from within each one; a copy at the stage root is silently ignored.
# Lite base (stage2) already provides systemd, apt, coreutils, openssh-server.
# Only add what the arlowe runtime actually needs at start-up.

# Python runtime. Two consumers, one interpreter (ADR-0008):
#   - system python3 directly: arlowe-identity-init, the identity CLI, boot-check.
#   - three venvs under /opt/arlowe/venvs/{voice,llm,stt}, each built by plan 07.1-04's
#     build-venvs.sh with --system-site-packages so they SHARE the apt modules below
#     rather than carrying private copies. arlowe-voice, arlowe-face, whisper-stt and
#     qwen-tokenizer name those venv interpreters in their Exec* stanzas.
# The apt-vs-pip rule (ADR-0008): anything with a compiled extension that Debian packages
# comes from apt -- it never has to build under the emulated cross-arch chroot, and one
# copy stays shared between the system-python and venv consumers. Everything Debian does
# not package is pinned pip residue in
# pi-gen/stage-arlowe/01-runtime/files/venv-requirements/{voice,llm,stt}.txt.
python3
python3-venv
python3-pip

# Audio (ALSA userspace for arlowe-voice and arlowe-face/WhisPlay audio output)
alsa-utils

# Networking (NetworkManager for wifi provisioning at pairing time, Phase 8)
network-manager

# Pi hardware GPIO + SPI access (WhisPlay display driver, WhisPlay audio HAT)
#
# python3-lgpio, NOT python3-rpi.gpio. RPi.GPIO 0.7.1 predates BCM2712 and cannot
# resolve the Pi 5's peripheral base: WhisPlay.py died at GPIO.setup() with
# "RuntimeError: Cannot determine SOC peripheral base address" on every boot, which
# reads like absent hardware but happens with the HAT attached. lgpio drives gpiochip
# devices and works on Pi 5. The RPi.GPIO *API* is still what the vendored driver
# calls, so rpi-lgpio (voice-nodeps.txt) supplies it as a shim over this package --
# leaving the Apache-2.0 upstream driver unmodified, which INSTALL.md requires.
# Both must change together.
python3-lgpio
# python3-rpi-lgpio provides the module named RPi.GPIO, backed by lgpio. Declared
# explicitly: the first build got it anyway as a transitive dependency, and a
# package the image depends on for its display driver should not arrive by
# accident. archive.raspberrypi.com carries 0.6-0~rpt1, so it is covered by the
# build-input manifest like every other apt package -- a stronger pin than the
# pip entry this replaces.
python3-rpi-lgpio
python3-spidev

# cmake — build-time only, for the native ax-llm build (01-runtime step 10).
# g++ and make are already present; cmake was the one missing piece, and without
# it /opt/arlowe/runtime/llm/bin/main_api_axcl_aarch64 is never produced and
# qwen-api exits 127 on every boot. A later pass can drop this from the shipped
# image once the build moves out of the chroot; it is listed here because the
# build currently runs inside it.
cmake

# ripgrep — used by the Phase 6-04 sanitize check script (faster than grep for
# scanning rootfs for nondeterminism markers such as build-host paths)
ripgrep

# dpkg tooling for axcl deb install (dpkg is present on Lite; listed explicitly
# for documentation clarity — not an extra install if already present)
dpkg

# Node.js for arlowe-dashboard is DELIBERATELY NOT an apt package -- see ADR-0008.
#
# The previous comment here asserted that bookworm's nodejs was new enough because
# dashboard/package.json declared a Node 18 floor. Both halves were false.
# runtime/dashboard/package.json has no `engines` field at all; the floor that actually
# binds comes from next@16.1.6's own engines.node, which is >= 20.9.0. Bookworm's
# candidate is 18.20.4 and bookworm-backports carries no nodejs at all (verified in an
# arm64 container -- the backports version table is empty). So apt cannot satisfy the
# floor at any pinning, and /usr/bin/node would be a wrong-version binary that the
# plan 07.1-03 SC1 path gate passes by construction, because the path exists.
#
# Resolution: `nodejs` and `npm` are dropped. The dashboard runs the vendored,
# SHA-256-pinned Node from third_party/node/manifest.yml, unpacked to the prefix that
# manifest declares, and units/arlowe-dashboard.service names that interpreter path
# explicitly (plan 07.1-04 applies it, 07.1-03's version-floor gate asserts it).
# One Node in the image, named by the unit, version-asserted by the gate.
# `npm` was unused either way: runtime/dashboard is pnpm (pnpm-lock.yaml, no package-lock.json).
#
# Fetching and unpacking that tarball inside the chroot needs these. Lite's base set is
# not guaranteed to carry them, and an undeclared unpack dependency is the same defect
# class as the missing python3-yaml below.
curl
ca-certificates
xz-utils

# First-boot models grow-to-fill (arlowe-grow-models). growpart lives in
# cloud-guest-utils; growpart relocates the GPT backup header on a GPT disk via
# sgdisk (gdisk). Without these the first-boot service dies with
# "growpart: command not found" (exit 127) and the models partition never grows.
cloud-guest-utils
gdisk

# Phase 7 device identity / PKI (ADR-0007). The identity CLI runs under system
# python3 -- /opt/arlowe/venvs is not populated by the image build -- so these must
# come from apt, not pip. python3-cryptography provides P-256 keygen + CSR building;
# python3-requests provides the mutual-TLS call to the IoT credentials provider.
python3-cryptography
python3-requests
# arlowe_config (imported transitively by the identity CLI) needs yaml + jsonschema.
# These were only ever installed on the CI host and in the CI test container, never
# in the rootfs, so `arlowe-identity init` died at import with ModuleNotFoundError on
# a real image -- arlowe-identity-init.service would have failed on every factory
# device and SC2 would never have passed.
python3-yaml
python3-jsonschema

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Phase 7.1 runtime substrate (ADR-0008). Every entry below is a compiled
# extension reachable from a shipping unit's Exec* stanza that Debian packages,
# so it comes from apt and is shared into the three venvs via
# --system-site-packages. Same shape as the Phase 7 block above: declare the
# transitive hop, name the importer, say why it is not pip.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# numpy -- voice/voice_client.py, face/audio_sync.py, lib/arlowe_audio.py.
# Also the ABI floor for every compiled wheel in the venvs: constraints.txt pins
# pip to THIS version so a venv cannot install a second numpy over the top. Two
# numpy builds in one image means the system-python consumers and the venv
# consumers no longer agree on array layout.
python3-numpy

# Pillow -- face/face.py renders the WhisPlay display (Image, ImageDraw,
# ImageFilter); runtime/recovery/arlowe-recovery.sh renders the recovery screen.
# NOTE: no font package is needed with it. arlowe-recovery.sh imports ImageFont
# but never calls it, and Pillow 9.4.0's built-in bitmap font accepts the
# anchor="mm" that the recovery draw.text calls pass -- verified in an arm64
# bookworm container. fonts-dejavu-core would be 2.9 MB of unreferenced data.
python3-pil

# scipy -- no runtime/ module imports it directly; it is a hard dependency of
# both noisereduce and openwakeword, which voice_client.py imports at module
# scope. This is the transitive hop that an "imports we grep for" audit misses.
python3-scipy

# scikit-learn + joblib -- there is NO `import sklearn` anywhere in runtime/,
# which is exactly why these were never declared. voice_client.py loads the
# wake-word verifier with pickle.load(); that pickle is a scikit-learn estimator
# produced by openwakeword.custom_verifier_model, and unpickling it imports
# sklearn (and joblib, its persistence layer) implicitly. Absent them, the load
# raises ModuleNotFoundError from inside pickle, not from an import line.
python3-sklearn
python3-joblib

# PyAudio -- voice/voice_client.py opens the capture stream; lib/arlowe_audio.py
# maps the resolved ALSA card to a PortAudio device index. Binds to portaudio19,
# which apt pulls in; the pip wheel would have to build it.
python3-pyaudio
